From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Brad Barnett <127794626+bdbarnett@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 12:00:00 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] dcd_dwc2: count down an IN transfer's bytes instead of
 reading XferSize back between packets.

In slave mode, epin_write_tx_fifo() writes as many packets of an IN
transfer as the endpoint's TX FIFO has room for. Before each packet it
read DIEPTSIZ again and took XferSize as the bytes still to write, up
to DIEPTSIZ's packet count.

The packet count is no bound. It falls when the core sends a packet,
not when one is written, so while the host is slow to read it still
counts packets that already sit in the FIFO, and the loop goes round
again after the transfer's last packet. Only XferSize then stops it, and
only if the read sees the packet that was just written. If it doesn't,
the loop writes one packet more than the transfer has. For the CDC TX
stream (a software FIFO, CFG_TUD_EDPT_DEDICATED_HWFIFO) that packet is
the next bytes the application has queued, taken out of the software
FIFO ahead of the transfer that should carry them; if fewer are
queued, the last word is padded with up to three zero bytes. For a
buffer transfer it is whatever lies past the end of the buffer.

On an ESP32-P4 (full-speed controller, native USB CDC console) long
console output read by a slow host now and then carried zero bytes
that were never printed, most often 3 of them right after a line and
before its "\r\n", which is where the CDC FIFO runs dry while the
board waits for the host (micropython-pydevices#163). A Python
os.dupterm() stream attached beside the CDC saw every write intact.

The loop now reads DIEPTSIZ once, counts the bytes down itself and
stops when the transfer has none left. handle_epin_slave() decides to
mask the TX FIFO empty interrupt from the same count, not from a read
taken after the writes. Where the read-back was already current this
changes nothing.

Based-on: micropython/tinyusb-espressif v0.21.0.1-micropython1 (5c5c660)
---
diff --git a/src/portable/synopsys/dwc2/dcd_dwc2.c b/src/portable/synopsys/dwc2/dcd_dwc2.c
index 740b795..f579d47 100644
--- a/src/portable/synopsys/dwc2/dcd_dwc2.c
+++ b/src/portable/synopsys/dwc2/dcd_dwc2.c
@@ -866,15 +866,21 @@ static uint16_t epin_write_tx_fifo(dwc2_regs_t *dwc2, uint8_t epnum) {
   dwc2_dep_t *const epin = &dwc2->ep[0][epnum];
   xfer_ctl_t *const xfer = XFER_CTL_BASE(epnum, TUSB_DIR_IN);
 
-  dwc2_ep_tsize_t tsiz           = {.value = epin->tsiz};
-  const uint16_t  remain_packets = tsiz.packet_count;
+  const dwc2_ep_tsize_t tsiz      = {.value = epin->tsiz};
+  const uint16_t        remain_packets = tsiz.packet_count;
+
+  // Bytes of this transfer not yet written to the FIFO. XferSize is read once here and counted down below, not read
+  // back between packets: read straight after a FIFO write it can still include the packet just written, and then
+  // the loop writes one packet more than the transfer has left. From a software FIFO that packet is the next
+  // transfer's bytes, zero-padded to a whole word; from a buffer it is whatever lies past the buffer's end. The
+  // packet count doesn't stop it: it also counts packets written but not yet sent, which pile up while the host is
+  // slow to read.
+  uint16_t remain_bytes = (uint16_t) tsiz.xfer_size;
 
   uint16_t total_bytes_written = 0;
   // Process every single packet (only whole packets can be written to fifo)
-  for (uint16_t i = 0; i < remain_packets; i++) {
-    tsiz.value                  = epin->tsiz;
-    const uint16_t remain_bytes = (uint16_t)tsiz.xfer_size;
-    const uint16_t xact_bytes   = tu_min16(remain_bytes, xfer->max_size);
+  for (uint16_t i = 0; i < remain_packets && remain_bytes > 0; i++) {
+    const uint16_t xact_bytes = tu_min16(remain_bytes, xfer->max_size);
 
     // Check if dtxfsts has enough space available
     if (xact_bytes > ((epin->dtxfsts & DTXFSTS_INEPTFSAV_Msk) << 2)) {
@@ -891,6 +897,7 @@ static uint16_t epin_write_tx_fifo(dwc2_regs_t *dwc2, uint8_t epnum) {
       xfer->buffer += xact_bytes;
       total_bytes_written += xact_bytes;
     }
+    remain_bytes -= xact_bytes;
   }
   return total_bytes_written;
 }
@@ -1014,11 +1021,12 @@ static void handle_epin_slave(uint8_t rhport, uint8_t epnum, dwc2_diepint_t diep
   // - 64 bytes or
   // - Half/Empty of TX FIFO size (configured by GAHBCFG.TXFELVL)
   if (diepint_bm.txfifo_empty && tu_bit_test(dwc2->diepempmsk, epnum)) {
-    epin_write_tx_fifo(dwc2, epnum);
+    // What is left to write, read before writing: read back after, it can still show the last packet as unwritten.
+    const dwc2_ep_tsize_t tsiz = {.value = epin->tsiz};
+    const uint16_t written = epin_write_tx_fifo(dwc2, epnum);
 
     // Turn off TXFE if all bytes are written.
-    dwc2_ep_tsize_t tsiz = {.value = epin->tsiz};
-    if (tsiz.xfer_size == 0) {
+    if (written >= tsiz.xfer_size) {
       dwc2->diepempmsk &= ~(1u << epnum);
     }
   }
